<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Eritrea</h1><p class="st-meta">1993 CE – present · capital Asmara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Africa's youngest state of the 20th century, won after a 30-year war with Ethiopia and ruled since independence by Isaias Afwerki.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in May 1991, ending the war that began in 1961 as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> dismantled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> federation. In a UN-monitored referendum in April 1993, 99.8 per cent voted for independence, which was declared on 24 May 1993, with EPLF leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-front-for-democracy-and-justice">People's Front for Democracy and Justice</a>, the only legal party. A constitution ratified in 1997 has never been implemented, and no national elections have been held. A border war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1998 to 2000 killed tens of thousands, and since then indefinite national service has driven many young Eritreans to emigrate. A 2001 crackdown silenced critics and the independent press.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> signed a peace agreement in 2018, ending two decades of hostility, and Eritrean forces later fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a> war. The country remains a one-party state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-eritrea.jpg" alt="State of Eritrea" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Eritrea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1993 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asmara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Isaias Afwerki</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tigrinya, Arabic, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eritrea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=state-of-eritrea" aria-label="State of Eritrea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5743 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Eritrea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6063 1863L6070 2096"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6070 2096h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6063 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6063 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Asmara. <a href="/atlas?year=2010&amp;focus=state-of-eritrea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
